  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue through this week.\n\n- Even hotter, drier, and windy conditions to end next week.\n\n- Potential cool down to near average temperatures next weekend,\n  though it remains dry. \n\nA ridge over the western U.S. is leaving western Montana and\ncentral Idaho hot and dry. The ridge is being sustained by the\nSouthwest Monsoon pattern that is currently going strong. However,\nthe ridge axis, or its center, is located in central and eastern\nMontana, and that is steering any of the monsoon moisture east of\nour region. As a result, very dry air from the northeast Pacific\nOcean region is being pulled in and then heated by the subsidence\nunder the ridge. This will continue for the rest of the week. The\nexception to the dryness is the southern portions of Lemhi County,\nID and much of southwest Montana where we expect thunderstorms\ntoday. But thunderstorms will be farther east the rest of the\nweekend and early next week. Overall, temperatures will remain\nabout 10 degrees above average for this time of year through\nWednesday or Thursday. \n\nThe air to the west is even drier, and it is helping to fuel the\nlarge wildfires in Oregon. There are also fires in Washington and\nBritish Columbia, but the direction of the flow means that most of\nthe smoke in our area is coming from Oregon, and we expect the\nsmoke to continue through this week at least. \n\nThe ridge builds back up towards the end of the week, just as a\ntrough tries to push into the Pacific Northwest by Friday. This\nsets up a classic break down of the ridge pattern, which means\nhot, dry, and windy conditions for Friday and Saturday. This is a\nmajor concern for fire weather, especially after a week of hot,\ndry conditions drying the landscape. \n\nOnce the break down of the ridge finishes, the region will cool\ndown to near average temperatures for this time of year over next\nweekend or into the following week. Looking at long term \nensembles, the vast majority of the members support this \ntransition, but there is a lot of variability in the timing. Some \nmembers have the cool down coming Saturday while others postpone \nit until as late as Monday. Either way, models aren't showing much\nmoisture coming with the front, so it will remain dry and breezy \nthrough the weekend.",
